# Gitleaks fingerprint baseline — known, assessed, INTENTIONAL findings.
#
# Rules for this file: one entry per finding, each with a comment saying what it
# is and why it is accepted (enforced by tests/unit/test_gitleaks_config.py — an
# unexplained fingerprint is indistinguishable from a suppressed real leak).
# Prefer fixing or rotating a credential over adding a line here.
#
# Refresh fingerprints with:  make check-secrets
#
# NOTE ON SHIPPED APPLICATION KEYS — read before "cleaning these up".
# CouchPotato is self-hosted software that is expected to work out of the box, so
# it deliberately ships public application keys for the third-party services it
# reads. themoviedb.py carries a pool of them (`ak`, base64) and falls back to
# them whenever the user has not configured their own; tmdb_charts.py logs
# "using built-in" when it does. These are ordinary application defaults, public
# in every copy of upstream CouchPotato, granting access to third-party *public*
# read APIs and to nothing of this project's.
#
# Removing one does not improve security. It silently costs every existing
# install the feature behind it. That mistake was made once here (the fanart.tv
# key was removed, which disabled all extra artwork for anyone who had not
# registered their own key) and reverted — see the comment in fanarttv.py.
# If you want a per-install key, ADD a setting that takes precedence; keep the
# shipped fallback.

# No fingerprints are currently suppressed.
#
# Be careful reading that as "there are no keys in the tree": the shipped
# fanart.tv key is base64-encoded (fanarttv.py `ak`, matching themoviedb.py's
# idiom), so gitleaks' hex-literal rules do not match it. The scan being clean
# reflects the encoding, not the absence of a shipped key. It is documented in
# the source, above, and in docs/technical-debt.md — nothing is hidden, and the
# encoding predates this repo as the upstream convention.
